  "question_type": "SA",
  "question": "If $$x=a\\sec\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\tan\\theta,$$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ without eliminating $\\theta$.",
  "answeroption": "A",
  "explanation": "<b>Differentiation with respect to the parameter</b><br>$$\\frac{dx}{d\\theta}=a\\sec\\theta\\tan\\theta$$<br>and $$\\frac{dy}{d\\theta}=b\\sec^2\\theta.$$<br><b>Parametric derivative</b><br>$$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{b\\sec^2\\theta}{a\\sec\\theta\\tan\\theta}=\\frac{b}{a}\\frac{\\sec\\theta}{\\tan\\theta}.$$<br><b>Simplification</b><br>Since $$\\frac{\\sec\\theta}{\\tan\\theta}=\\frac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$$ therefore $$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{b}{a\\sin\\theta}.$$",
  "explanation_image": [],
  "options": {
    "A": "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{b}{a\\sin\\theta}$",
    "B": "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{b}{a\\cos\\theta}$",
    "C": "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{a}{b\\sin\\theta}$",
    "D": "$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{b\\sin\\theta}{a}$"
  },
